t>self.__next_f.push([1,"{\"@context\":\"https://schema.org\",\"@type\":\"NewsArticle\",\"mainEntityOfPage\":\"https://www.lancs.live/news/motoring/motoring-news/warning-easter-food-can-trigger-31393013\",\"inLanguage\":\"en-GB\",\"headline\":\"Warning Easter food can trigger a false positive breathalyser test result\",\"name\":\"Warning Easter food can trigger a false positive breathalyser test result\",\"description\":\"You may want to wait a few minutes after having one before getting behind the wheel\",\"articleBody\":\"If you’re hitting the road this Easter, then be wary of enjoying a hot cross bun or two before your trip. Motoring experts from  Ripe Motorhome Insurance  have warned of the lesser-known side effects of hot cross buns on a breathalyser test. Alan Thomas, CEO of Ripe Motorhome Insurance said: “Just one bite of a hot cross bun can increase your blood alcohol level from 0 up to 0.018. \\\"Foods that have started to ferment, such as hot cross buns containing yeast, can create trace amounts of mouth alcohol, which may show up on a breathalyser test. “While this isn’t reflective of the actual blood alcohol level in your system, this means that consuming a hot cross bun or two could get you into hot water if pulled over and lead to an unnecessary follow-up test which is the last thing you’d want over the Easter weekend!” But it’s not just hot cross buns which can cause a false-positive test. Other lesser-known foods and drinks which could lead to false readings: Fermented drinks like kombucha Energy drinks Fermented foods like sauerkraut, kimchi and yoghurts Vanilla extract If you are partial to a hot cross bun during the spring season, make sure to wash it down with a glass of water before driving if you’re worried about a false-positive reading. Fermented foods like sauerkraut, kimchi, kombucha, kefir, soy sauce, or even ripe fruits can contain small amounts of alcohol due to the fermentation process. When you eat them or drink something like kombucha, trace alcohol might be present in your mouth—this is called mouth alcohol. Mouth alcohol can skew breathalyser results because most breathalysers are designed to detect ethanol on your breath, assuming it’s from the lungs (indicating blood alcohol content). If you’ve just consumed something fermented, and a breath test is taken immediately after, the residual alcohol in your mouth can cause a false positive. After a few minutes (usually 10–15), the mouth alcohol dissipates, and the breathalyser will give a more accurate reading. Low-level false positives might happen with certain very sensitive devices. Police-grade breathalysers usually involve a waiting period or multiple tests to avoid this kind of error. Consumer devices (like personal breathalysers or ignition interlocks) might be more prone to picking up on these trace levels.\",\"datePublished\":\"2025-04-10T09:47:50Z\",\"dateModified\":\"2025-04-10T09:47:50Z\",\"image\":[\"https://i2-prod.lancs.live/article31393021.ece/ALTERNATES/s1200d/0_GettyImages-150639036.jpg\",\"https://i2-prod.lancs.live/article31393021.ece/ALTERNATES/s1200b/0_GettyImages-150639036.jpg\",\"https://i2-prod.lancs.live/article31393021.ece/ALTERNATES/s1200c/0_GettyImages-150639036.jpg\"],\"author\":[{\"@type\":\"Person\",\"name\":\"Neil Shaw\",\"sameAs\":\"https://www.lancs.live/authors/neil-shaw/\"}],\"publisher\":{\"@type\":\"NewsMediaOrganization\",\"name\":\"Lancs Live\",\"url\":\"https://www.lancs.live\",\"publishingPrinciples\":\"https://www.lancs.live/about-us/\",\"logo\":{\"@type\":\"ImageObject\",\"url\":\"https://www.lancs.live/nav-web-static/main-0be4de3fe683999b5c7bc0a5c9ed622c4eeb7147/public/assets/accrington/logos/masthead-accrington-default.svg\",\"width\":600,\"height\":160},\"sameAs\":[\"https://www.facebook.com/lancslive\",\"https://x.com/LiveLancs\",\"https://www.instagram.com/LancsLive\"]},\"speakable\":{\"@type\":\"SpeakableSpecification\",\"cssSelector\":[\"#article-body h2[data-testid='leadtext']\"]}}"])</script><